{"detail": {"id": "hoids-hop8-4", "book": "HoIDS", "chapter": 8, "exercise_number": "Hands-On Problem 8.4", "title": "Gradient Descent: 2014\u20132015 movies and social media", "category": "Gradient descent", "source_page": "p. 233", "kind": "computational", "required_dataset": "csm_movies", "origin": "textbook", "description": "Use the conventional and social media movies dataset (OA 8.6). (a) What can you tell about a movie's rating from its budget and aggregated social-media followers? (b) If you add likes, dislikes and comments, does the prediction improve? (c) Among all the factors, which is the best predictor of rating? With it, use gradient descent to find the optimal intercept and gradient.", "concepts": ["multiple linear regression", "R\u00b2 vs adjusted R\u00b2", "choosing a predictor", "gradient descent", "missing values"], "understand": "Compare models on the same movies; adjusted R\u00b2 rewards a predictor only if it adds more than chance. Then fit one predictor by gradient descent and check that it lands on the least-squares line.", "questions": [{"id": "a", "prompt": "Rating from budget and aggregate followers.", "answer": null, "explanation": ""}, {"id": "b", "prompt": "Add likes, dislikes and comments: does the prediction improve?", "answer": null, "explanation": ""}, {"id": "c", "prompt": "The best single predictor, and gradient descent's intercept and gradient for it.", "answer": null, "explanation": ""}], "interpretation": "Budget and social-media interaction explain only a small part of a movie's rating; the extra interaction variables help, and budget is the single best predictor.", "common_mistakes": ["Comparing R\u00b2 of models fitted on different rows (missing values differ between columns).", "Judging a model only by R\u00b2, which always rises when variables are added; look at adjusted R\u00b2.", "Running gradient descent on raw budgets (hundreds of millions): scale the feature first."], "python_code": "import numpy as np, pandas as pd\nm = pd.read_excel(\"2014 and 2015 CSM dataset.xlsx\")\ncols = [\"Budget\", \"Aggregate Followers\", \"Likes\", \"Dislikes\", \"Comments\"]\nd = m.dropna(subset=[\"Ratings\", *cols])\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nfor X in ([\"Budget\", \"Aggregate Followers\"], cols):\n    print(X, LinearRegression().fit(d[X], d[\"Ratings\"]).score(d[X], d[\"Ratings\"]))\nmu, sd = d[\"Budget\"].mean(), d[\"Budget\"].std(ddof=0)\nx, y = ((d[\"Budget\"] - mu) / sd).to_numpy(), d[\"Ratings\"].to_numpy()\nt0 = t1 = 0.0\nfor _ in range(5000):                        # batch gradient descent, cost J = (1/2m) * sum(error^2)\n    e = t0 + t1 * x - y\n    t0, t1 = t0 - 0.1 * e.mean(), t1 - 0.1 * (e * x).mean()\nprint(\"intercept\", t0 - t1 * mu / sd, \"slope\", t1 / sd)\n", "parameters": {"learning_rate": 0.1, "iterations": 5000}, "notes": ["Data: UCI dataset 424 (CC BY 4.0), the file named in OA 8.6. The file has 231 movies (the UCI page says 217).", "Missing values (aggregate followers 35, screens 10, budget 1): every model uses the same complete rows for the five factors, so R\u00b2 values compare fairly."]}, "solve": {"answers": [{"id": "a", "answer": {"r2": 0.10485702670269492, "adj_r2": 0.095532620730848, "n": 195, "p_budget": 7.816488049661026e-06, "p_followers": 0.7047211214146102}, "explanation": "ratings ~ budget + followers explains only 10.5% of the variation (n = 195 movies). Budget is significant (p < 0.001); followers are not significant (p = 0.70)."}, {"id": "b", "answer": {"r2": 0.21926089351802147, "adj_r2": 0.198606419801567, "gain_r2": 0.11440386681532655, "gain_adj_r2": 0.10307379907071901}, "explanation": "Adding likes, dislikes and comments raises R\u00b2 from 0.105 to 0.219 (adjusted 0.096 \u2192 0.199): yes, it improves the prediction, though most of the variation in ratings is still unexplained."}, {"id": "c", "answer": {"best_predictor": "budget", "correlation": 0.32277779353122754, "gd_intercept": 6.106385633141369, "gd_slope": 5.700974527283809e-09, "ols_intercept": 6.106409977709401, "ols_slope": 5.700997255583417e-09, "iterations_run": 118, "stop_reason": "converged"}, "explanation": "The best single predictor is budget (r = 0.323). Gradient descent on the standardized budget finds rating = 6.106 + 5.701e-09\u00b7budget after 118 iterations, the same line as least squares (6.106 + 5.701e-09\u00b7budget); per $1 million of budget, +0.006 rating points."}], "model_a": {"n": 195, "response": "ratings", "predictors": ["budget", "aggregate_followers"], "r2": 0.10485702670269492, "adj_r2": 0.095532620730848, "F": 11.24543772753874, "F_p": 2.4080808334113203e-05, "rse": 0.9525227597339957, "coefficients": [{"term": "(Intercept)", "estimate": 6.094096857747614, "std_error": 0.09827245737375259, "t": 62.01225674626586, "p": 6.026986841228111e-129}, {"term": "budget", "estimate": 5.622578106561334e-09, "std_error": 1.2235605413124207e-09, "t": 4.59525942257865, "p": 7.816488049661026e-06}, {"term": "aggregate_followers", "estimate": 5.378095808262845e-09, "std_error": 1.4170780560991775e-08, "t": 0.37952008254698755, "p": 0.7047211214146102}], "fitted": [6.122610637479114, 6.4416452463077265, 6.254126665006722, 6.715635207888454, 6.124122261836515, 6.098575356501455, 6.3628475971348335, 6.2072510949866695, 6.252204780957256, 6.281069242260252, 6.424835138141561, 6.264852297164767, 6.235086800736787, 6.487158368647136, 6.122291022064275, 6.1291929846264726, 6.131131471416748, 6.134079301416882, 7.022896788872725, 6.149475809784403, 6.331998839578638, 6.117151870233727, 6.268787354180047, 6.159103581212729, 6.099731170859229, 7.093121245203391, 7.050548238785183, 6.266778731083287, 6.13350619001517, 6.577555435487838, 6.297998057598758, 6.4898043620990755, 6.246697046708585, 6.382658918349457, 6.167666797128182, 7.125086878199887, 6.206664048938719, 6.125099240783899, 6.302826206794452, 6.486586013879784, 6.104856808425583, 6.107557789433741, 7.003766393958879, 6.548078019730401, 7.064724899335763, 6.1261550529053